A||||6||Kelly||Engine Problem||||
Z||000000||Kelly||05-01-99||04:51 PM||||I've got an 8x model Fiero. I've had it in the shop several times and they can't seem to fix it. When I start it up int he morning, everything seems to run so so. Then when it get warmed up, it starts choking and backfiring, and looses power. Anyone have any ideas as what can be causing it. It sounds like it flooding out or the timing might be off. But according to the shop, there's nothing wrong with it.
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||05-01-99||05:31 PM||||It could be a number of things... The most common are the ignition module or the tach filter. Try disconnecting the tach filter first and see if the problem still remains. If it does, you might want to try to replace the ignition module. 4 out of 5 times the ignition module is bad.
Z||000002||HF6582||05-02-99||08:39 AM||||my car had the same problem, and I couldn't<br>figure out what was wrong with it.<br>replaced just about everything that I<br>could.  then the fuel pump went completely<br>out. replaced it and everything is fine now.<br>check your fuel pressure or have a shop do<br>it if you don't have a guage.
Z||000003||grizco||05-17-99||04:57 PM||||I don't know if your car has a distributor of if it uses the DIS system but if it has a distributor have it checked. I just recently had a problem with my 84 SE were it would lurch and run rough after it had warmed up between 2,000 and 3,000 RPM. After replacing the O2 sensor and a few other sensors that I was getting codes for it still ran the same. Finally I checked the distributor and found it was worn. After replacing it and retiming the motor all the drivability problems i was experiencing went away.
Z||000004||Stroker||06-12-99||12:46 PM||||If your car is a v-6, then change the pickup coil in the distributor every 10,000-30,000 miles!! Especially if it is a STROKER motor!!!<br>I have learned this the hard way!!<br>Trust me, keep an extra with you!!!
Z||000005||mshill||09-10-1999||02:26 PM||||Stroker,  my 86' SE has 188K and I replaced the cap/rotor.  The pickup coil had green corrosion on the connectors.  While attempting to clean it the pickup coil plastic housing virtually disintegrated.  I cannot believe the cheap crap that GM/Pontiac used in some very critical places.<P>I am rebuilding my distributor tonight.||||reg||
Z||000006||chris6878||09-10-1999||09:21 PM||||I had the same problem with my 84 4-banger. It would run fine until it warmed up and then would start stuttering and back firing. I replaced this and that and even replaced the computer. Well, it didnt fix the problem. Then one day i was driving and the car starts stuttering and then completely quits. I couldnt get it to start back up. Me and my dad then noticed when the motor was being cranked there wasnt any gas being sprayed by the injector. Well, to make a long story short it was the fuel pump. I could drive the car until it warmed and it would quit dead. Let the fuel pump cool for about 10 - 15 minutes and it would fire right up with no problems. I changed fuel pumps and it has ran good ever since....  <P>Sorry for the long story, hope it helps...<P>chris||||reg||7
